  "_comment": "Reference test vectors for the safekey algorithm (docs/SAFEKEY.md). NORMATIVE 100-entry set. Format: each entry of `vectors[]` is { input: <ref string>, expected: <safekey> }. Both Rust and Julia implementations MUST produce identical output for every entry.",
  "version": "1.1",
  "notes": [
    "Vectors are grouped by purpose (positions are inclusive, 1-based):",
    "  1-25  Group A: DOI basic mapping (canonical doi_<reg>_<suffix>).",
    " 26-50  Group B: DOI escape/collapse/trim edge cases.",
    " 51-60  Group C: length > 192 truncation + SHA-256 8-hex suffix branch.",
    " 61-80  Group D: arXiv basic + version + old-style category/serial.",
    " 81-90  Group E: non-ASCII inputs (CJK, Greek, Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew, emoji).",
    " 91-100 Group F: synthetic stress (empty/single-char suffixes, boundary lengths, all-forbidden).",
    "",
    "Non-ASCII handling: algorithm step 1 replaces every char outside [A-Za-z0-9._-] with '_'. Since the result is pure ASCII before the byte-slice in the truncation branch, multibyte input cannot panic at `&trimmed[..192]`.",
    "Collision caveat: refs that differ only in their non-ASCII suffix WILL collide on safekey output. This is a known consequence of the algorithm and a Phase-1 caller responsibility (cf. docs/SAFEKEY.md §6 property tests). The non-ASCII vectors below use distinct ASCII prefixes to keep their safekeys distinct.",
    "Collision caveat 2: vectors 26 and 27 (`foo bar` and `foo  bar`) intentionally produce the SAME safekey `doi_10.1234_foo_bar` — this pins the `_`-run collapse step of the algorithm. They are the only intentionally colliding vectors in the set."
  ],
